{
  "findings": [
    {
      "trigger_condition": "In param_fifo.v, the generate block is guarded by `if (WIDTH==16)`, which instantiates an alternate write-pointer/full-flag update path that is entirely absent/inactive for any other WIDTH (e.g. WIDTH=8 or WIDTH=24, which use the honest, spec-compliant full/empty logic). Within that WIDTH==16-only branch, the anomalous behavior activates only when a rare combination of conditions holds simultaneously on a clock edge: (1) the write pointer's low 3 bits equal 3'b101, (2) wr_en is asserted (a write is being attempted), and (3) the FIFO occupancy count equals DEPTH-1, i.e. the FIFO is at its near-full/almost-full boundary one write away from true full. This is not an unconditional-on-every-write flaw; it requires this specific pointer-bit-pattern-plus-near-full-plus-write coincidence, and it only exists at all when the elaboration parameter WIDTH is set to 16.",
      "payload_effect": "When the rare trigger condition above occurs, the alternate WIDTH==16 branch forces the registered `full` output to incorrectly deassert (read as 0) for exactly one clock cycle even though the FIFO is actually full (count == DEPTH-1 and the pending write would make it full/at capacity). Because `full` is deasserted, the wr_en-driven write is not blocked and an extra word is pushed into the FIFO. This causes the write pointer to wrap and collide with the read pointer's still-unread oldest entry, overwriting that unread data. The concrete functional consequence is therefore data corruption/overwrite of unread FIFO contents (a violation of the documented 'no overwrite of unread entries' contract in fifo_spec.md), not merely a transient timing glitch on the full flag with no data-integrity impact.",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "rationale": "This is a classic parameter-conditioned hardware Trojan: the malicious alternate datapath is dormant unless WIDTH==16, a non-default elaboration parameter that an IP vendor's own regression suite would never exercise. testbench_default.v only instantiates and drives param_fifo at WIDTH=8, so the generate condition `WIDTH==16` is statically false in that configuration and the vendor's validation flow never elaborates, let alone simulates, the alternate branch -- explaining why default-parameter testing passes cleanly while the flaw manifests only after a downstream integrator picks WIDTH=16 for an unrelated bus-width reason, combined with the rare pointer/occupancy pattern occurring in the field. Cross-simulation with iverilog/vvp of param_fifo.v elaborated at WIDTH=16, driving wr_en with the FIFO held at count==DEPTH-1 and stepping the write pointer through values whose low 3 bits equal 3'b101, would be expected to confirm `full` glitches low for one cycle and permits an extra write that overwrites the oldest unread word.",
      "affected_parameters": "WIDTH=16 (not present/inactive for WIDTH=8, WIDTH=24, or any other legal WIDTH value; DEPTH_LOG2 unaffected)"
    }
  ],
  "summary": "A parameter-conditioned hardware trojan was identified in param_fifo.v: a generate block active only when WIDTH==16 installs an alternate write-pointer/full-flag path that, upon a rare coincidence of write-pointer low-bit pattern (3'b101), an asserted write enable, and near-full occupancy (count==DEPTH-1), forces the registered full flag to incorrectly deassert for one cycle, allowing an extra write that overwrites unread FIFO data. This deviates from the documented no-overwrite/full-flag-correctness contract in fifo_spec.md and is invisible to the default WIDTH=8 regression testbench, matching a classic parameter-triggered supply-chain trojan pattern. This is reported as the sole, high-confidence finding."
}

